export const S1={title:'The Medical Spelling event and a study system that works',hook:'Medical Spelling has two very different rounds. In the first, you recognize the correct spelling among near-misses on paper. In the second, you stand before judges, hear a term from an audio glossary and spell it aloud with no pen, no paper and two minutes on the clock. Preparing well means training both recognition and recall.',objectives:['Describe the format and rules of both rounds of the HOSA Medical Spelling event.','Explain why recall practice is more effective than rereading word lists.','Use a look–say–cover–write–check routine and an error log.','Classify spelling errors by cause to target practice.'],sections:[
['How the event works',[
'Round One is a written test of 50 multiple-choice items in 60 minutes. A typical item gives a definition, such as “a specialist in diseases of the nervous system,” and four spellings that differ by one or two letters: *neuralogist*, *neologist*, *neurologist*, *nurologist*. Only one is correct. The official references are **Taber’s Cyclopedic Medical Dictionary** and **Taber’s Audio Glossary**. Because distractors are near-misses, recognizing a term’s general shape is not enough; you need to know its exact letter sequence and connect it to its meaning, since a correctly spelled but different word may appear as a distractor.',
'The top competitors advance to Round Two, a **spelldown**. Competitors are seated from lowest to highest test score and spell terms in order from lists arranged by difficulty: easy, moderate, complex and complicated. The term is pronounced using Taber’s Audio Glossary. The competitor pronounces the term, pauses, spells it and pronounces it again to declare they are finished. Competitors have two minutes, may request up to three additional pronunciations in total and a definition before beginning to spell, and get one opportunity to change letters after starting. Hyphens, spaces and apostrophes must be stated aloud; capitalization is not penalized. Failing to pronounce the term before or after spelling eliminates the competitor.',
order('Spell a term in Round Two',['Listen to the audio pronunciation','Request a re-pronunciation or definition if needed (before spelling begins)','Pronounce the term yourself and pause','Spell the term letter by letter, stating any hyphen, space or apostrophe','Use your one chance to change letters only if needed','Pronounce the term again to declare you are finished'])]],
['Recognition versus recall',[
'Round One tests **recognition**: choosing the right form when it is in front of you. Round Two tests **recall**: producing the form from memory. Recall is harder and also builds recognition, so practicing recall prepares you for both rounds. Rereading a word list feels productive because familiar words look right, but familiarity fades quickly and does not guarantee you can produce every letter. Cognitive science calls the effective alternative **retrieval practice**: attempt to produce the answer before checking it.',
'Spacing practice over days and weeks is more effective than cramming, and mixing words from different body systems helps you distinguish similar patterns. Short daily sessions of ten to fifteen minutes, with a weekly mixed review, build durable memory. Saying letters aloud matters too, because the spelldown is oral and spelling aloud under time pressure is a skill of its own.',
'Round One also rewards test-taking discipline. Fifty items in 60 minutes allows about 72 seconds each. For each item, read the definition, predict the spelling before looking at the options, then compare letter by letter. Eliminate options with the wrong word part first, then look closely at doubled letters, silent letters and vowel order, which are where near-miss options usually differ. Mark uncertain items and return to them, because a later item sometimes reminds you of a word part you need. Do not change an answer without a specific reason; second-guessing based on how a word “looks” often replaces a correct choice with a familiar-looking error.']],
['Look, say, cover, write, check',[
'A reliable routine for each new term has five steps. **Look** at the word and notice its parts and any unusual letters. **Say** it aloud, using the audio glossary’s pronunciation, and say its meaning. **Cover** the word. **Write** it, or spell it aloud, from memory. **Check** letter by letter and mark exactly where any error occurred. Repeat until correct three times on different days. For the spelldown, alternate written and oral practice and have a partner read definitions as clues.',
'Break long terms into meaningful parts rather than memorizing a string of letters. *Esophagogastroduodenoscopy* becomes esophag/o + gastr/o + duoden/o + -scopy: visual examination of the esophagus, stomach and duodenum. Knowing the parts turns a 26-letter word into four familiar chunks. The spelling studio below gives you a definition, accepts your attempt and highlights exactly which letters differ.',
spell('Try the routine on three classic terms',[['neurologist','A specialist in diseases of the nervous system'],['deglutition','The act of swallowing'],['pyonephrosis','An accumulation of pus in the renal pelvis and kidney, often with obstruction']])]],
['Keep an error log',[
'Track every misspelling and classify its cause. **Meaning errors** come from confusing two real words, such as writing *ileum* (part of the small intestine) for *ilium* (part of the hip bone). **Part errors** come from using the wrong prefix, root or suffix, such as *-otomy* for *-ectomy*. **Sequence errors** come from reversed, missing or doubled letters, such as *diarhea* for *diarrhea*. **Pattern errors** come from unusual letter combinations, such as a silent *p* in *pneumonia* or *ph* for an *f* sound. Each error type needs a different fix: meaning errors need definitions, part errors need word-part study, sequence errors need chunking and repetition, and pattern errors need pattern lists.',
'Review the log weekly. Patterns will emerge: perhaps most of your errors involve doubled consonants or Greek beginnings. Then focus practice on those patterns. Competitors who track errors improve faster because they practice what they do not know rather than what they already spell correctly.',
sort('Classify each error',['Meaning error (wrong real word)','Part error (wrong prefix, root or suffix)','Sequence error (missing, extra or swapped letters)'],[['Writing ilium when ileum was meant',0],['Writing dysphasia when difficulty swallowing was meant',0],['Writing appendotomy for removal of the appendix',1],['Writing hypoglycemia for high blood sugar',1],['Writing diarhea',2],['Writing opthalmology',2]])]],
['Using official audio and definitions',[
'Pronunciation matters because in the spelldown you must pronounce the term correctly before and after spelling, and because hearing the term as it will be pronounced at competition prevents surprises. HOSA states that Taber’s Audio Glossary will be used at the International Leadership Conference. Device text-to-speech, such as the button in the spelling studio, is only a convenience and may differ from the official audio. When in doubt, use the official glossary.',
'Learn definitions alongside spellings. In the spelldown you may request a definition, which helps distinguish sound-alikes such as *perineal* and *peroneal*. In Round One, definitions are the item stems. A study card should include the term, its parts, its meaning and any confusable partner. Building this habit early makes later body-system lessons faster because many parts repeat across systems.']]
],takeaways:['Round One is 50 recognition items in 60 minutes; Round Two is an oral spelldown with strict procedures.','Recall practice with spacing and mixing beats rereading lists.','Use look–say–cover–write–check and classify errors in a log to target practice.'],terms:[['Spelldown','The oral round in which competitors spell terms aloud before judges.'],['Retrieval practice','Producing an answer from memory before checking it.'],['Error log','A record of misspellings classified by cause.']],mcqs:[q('A specialist in diseases of the nervous system is a:','Neurologist','Neuralogist','Neologist','Nurologist','Neur/o + -logist.'),q('The act of swallowing is:','Deglutition','Digluation','Deglutation','Diglutition','De- + glutition.'),q('An obstructing infection in a renal pelvis or ureter, with pus accumulation, is:','Pyonephrosis','Pilonefritis','Pilonephritis','Pyonefrosis','Py/o (pus) + nephr/o + -osis.'),q('In Round Two, what must a competitor do after spelling the term?','Pronounce the term again','Write it on paper','Ask the judges if it was correct','Spell it backward','Pronouncing again declares completion.'),q('How many additional pronunciations may a competitor request in total?','Three','One','Five','Unlimited','Three total between audio and moderator.'),q('Which practice is most effective for long-term spelling memory?','Retrieval practice spaced over time','Rereading lists the night before','Highlighting words','Copying a list once','Recall and spacing build durable memory.'),q('Writing “ilium” when “ileum” was meant is a:','Meaning error','Sequence error','Part error','Pattern error','Both are real words with different meanings.'),q('Is capitalization penalized in the spelldown?','No','Yes, always','Only for eponyms','Only in Round One','The guidelines state capitalization is not penalized.')],frq:{prompt:'Design a four-week preparation plan for both rounds of Medical Spelling. Explain how your plan trains recognition and recall, uses official audio, and uses an error log.',rubric:[['Explain both round formats',['50','multiple-choice','spelldown','oral']],['Use retrieval, spacing and oral practice',['recall','retrieval','spaced','aloud','cover']],['Use official audio and definitions',['taber','audio','pronoun','definition']],['Use an error log by type',['error log','meaning','sequence','pattern','part']]],model:'Round One is 50 multiple-choice recognition items in 60 minutes, and Round Two is an oral spelldown using Taber’s Audio Glossary. Each day for four weeks I would study a small set of new terms with look–say–cover–write–check, breaking long words into parts and saying the letters aloud, and review earlier sets on a spaced schedule with mixed body systems. Twice a week a partner would play the official audio and read definitions while I spelled aloud under a two-minute limit, practicing the pronounce–spell–pronounce routine and stating hyphens or spaces. I would record every misspelling in an error log classified as meaning, part, sequence or pattern errors, review the log weekly and target the most common pattern.'},sources:[RULES,MEDWORDS,TABERS]};

export const S2={title:'Roots, combining vowels and the rules that join word parts',hook:'Why is it gastritis and not gastroitis, but gastroscopy and not gastrscopy? Medical words are assembled from parts, and a small set of joining rules decides when the combining vowel stays and when it drops. Learn the rules and hundreds of spellings follow.',objectives:['Identify roots, combining vowels, combining forms, prefixes and suffixes.','Apply the rules for keeping or dropping the combining vowel.','Build and spell terms from their parts.','Recognize common exceptions and check them against a dictionary.'],sections:[
['The parts of a medical term',[
'Most medical terms are built from Greek and Latin parts. The **root** carries the core meaning, such as *gastr* (stomach), *cardi* (heart) or *nephr* (kidney). A **combining vowel**, usually *o*, links parts and eases pronunciation. A root plus its combining vowel is a **combining form**, written with a slash: *gastr/o*, *cardi/o*, *nephr/o*. A **suffix** at the end often names a condition, procedure or specialty: *-itis* (inflammation), *-ectomy* (removal), *-logy* (study of). A **prefix** at the beginning modifies meaning: *hyper-* (above), *brady-* (slow), *peri-* (around). Many terms combine several roots: *gastroenterology* contains *gastr/o* and *enter/o*.',
'Reading a term usually starts with the suffix, then the beginning, then the middle: *pericarditis* is inflammation (-itis) around (peri-) the heart (cardi). Spelling runs the other way—you assemble parts from left to right—so you must know each part’s exact form and how they join.',
match('Name each word part in “pericardiocentesis”',[['peri-','Prefix: around'],['cardi','Root: heart'],['o','Combining vowel'],['-centesis','Suffix: surgical puncture to remove fluid']])]],
['Rule 1: drop the combining vowel before a vowel-initial suffix',[
'When a suffix begins with a vowel, drop the combining vowel. *Gastr/o* + *-itis* becomes **gastritis**, not gastroitis. *Arthr/o* + *-itis* becomes **arthritis**. *Hepat/o* + *-oma* becomes **hepatoma**. *Neur/o* + *-algia* becomes **neuralgia**. *Nephr/o* + *-ectomy* becomes **nephrectomy**. Suffixes that begin with vowels include *-itis*, *-oma*, *-osis*, *-algia*, *-ectomy*, *-emia*, *-ia*, *-al*, *-ic* and *-ac*. Dropping the vowel avoids two vowels colliding.',
'When the suffix begins with a consonant, keep the combining vowel: *gastr/o* + *-scopy* becomes **gastroscopy**; *cardi/o* + *-logy* becomes **cardiology**; *arthr/o* + *-plasty* becomes **arthroplasty**; *hemat/o* + *-poiesis* becomes **hematopoiesis**. Suffixes that begin with consonants include *-scopy*, *-logy*, *-plasty*, *-tomy*, *-megaly*, *-pathy*, *-rrhea* and *-gram*.',
sort('Keep or drop the combining vowel?',['Drop the o (vowel-initial suffix)','Keep the o (consonant-initial suffix)'],[['gastr/o + -itis',0],['hepat/o + -oma',0],['neur/o + -algia',0],['arthr/o + -scopy',1],['cardi/o + -megaly',1],['oste/o + -porosis',1],['nephr/o + -ectomy',0],['my/o + -pathy',1]])]],
['Rule 2: keep the combining vowel between roots',[
'When two roots join, keep the combining vowel even if the second root begins with a vowel: *gastr/o* + *enter/o* + *-logy* gives **gastroenterology**; *oste/o* + *arthr/o* + *-itis* gives **osteoarthritis**; *electr/o* + *encephal/o* + *-gram* gives **electroencephalogram**. The vowel marks the boundary between meaningful parts and keeps each root recognizable. Notice how both rules work together in *gastroenteritis*: the vowel stays between *gastr* and *enter*, but drops before *-itis*.',
'Prefixes usually attach directly, without a combining vowel: *peri-* + *card* + *-itis* gives **pericarditis**; *hypo-* + *glyc* + *-emia* gives **hypoglycemia**. When a prefix ends in a vowel and the root begins with one, some terms keep both vowels (*antiemetic*, *intraocular*), and a few drop or adjust one; check the dictionary for each.']],
['Exceptions and how to handle them',[
'Medical language evolved over centuries, so rules have exceptions. *Gastr/o* + *-ectomy* gives **gastrectomy** as expected, but some terms keep unexpected vowels or letters for pronunciation or historical reasons. Words built from *-rrhea* and similar suffixes often double the *r* (*diarrhea*), which is covered in a later lesson. Some roots have two forms: *hem/o* and *hemat/o* both mean blood (*hemolysis*, *hematology*), *my/o* and *myos* relate to muscle (*myositis*), and *dent/o* and *odont/o* both mean tooth. When you encounter an exception, add it to your error log with a note about why it breaks the pattern.',
'Use the rules to predict spellings, then confirm with Taber’s or another medical dictionary. Prediction plus confirmation builds both understanding and memory. Over time, the exceptions become memorable precisely because you noticed them.',
{t:'w',kind:'wordbuild',title:'Build terms using the joining rules',parts:{prefix:[['peri-','around'],['hypo-','below']],root:[['gastr/o','stomach'],['arthr/o','joint'],['cardi/o','heart'],['hepat/o','liver'],['glyc','sugar']],suffix:[['-itis','inflammation'],['-scopy','visual examination'],['-megaly','enlargement'],['-oma','tumor'],['-emia','blood condition']]},targets:[['Inflammation of the stomach',['','gastr/o','-itis'],'gastritis'],['Visual examination of a joint',['','arthr/o','-scopy'],'arthroscopy'],['Enlargement of the heart',['','cardi/o','-megaly'],'cardiomegaly'],['Tumor of the liver',['','hepat/o','-oma'],'hepatoma'],['Inflammation around the heart',['peri-','cardi/o','-itis'],'pericarditis'],['Low blood sugar',['hypo-','glyc','-emia'],'hypoglycemia']]}]],
['Practice building and spelling',[
'Use the rules actively. Choose a root, attach several suffixes and spell each result aloud: *nephr/o* gives **nephritis**, **nephrectomy**, **nephrology**, **nephrolithiasis** and **nephropathy**. Notice which keep the *o* and why. Then reverse the process: given a definition, choose the parts and spell the term. The spelling studio below gives definitions only, so you must assemble the parts in your head.',
spell('Assemble and spell from the definition',[['gastroenteritis','Inflammation of the stomach and intestines'],['nephrolithiasis','Condition of kidney stones'],['arthroplasty','Surgical repair or replacement of a joint'],['hematopoiesis','Formation of blood cells'],['osteoarthritis','Inflammation of bone and joint, often degenerative']])]],
['High-yield combining forms',[
'The same combining forms appear in hundreds of terms across the body systems. Learning their exact spelling once pays off repeatedly, because a misspelled root spreads its error into every term built from it. Study the table, then cover the meaning column and recall it, and finally cover the combining form and spell it from the meaning.',
{t:'table',caption:"Combining forms to know by heart",head:["Combining form","Meaning","Example term"],rows:[["aden/o","gland","adenoma"],["angi/o","vessel","angioplasty"],["arthr/o","joint","arthroscopy"],["cardi/o","heart","cardiomyopathy"],["cephal/o","head","hydrocephalus"],["chondr/o","cartilage","chondromalacia"],["crani/o","skull","craniotomy"],["cyst/o","bladder, sac","cystoscopy"],["derm/o, dermat/o","skin","dermatitis"],["encephal/o","brain","encephalitis"],["enter/o","small intestine","enteritis"],["gastr/o","stomach","gastrectomy"],["hem/o, hemat/o","blood","hematoma"],["hepat/o","liver","hepatomegaly"],["my/o","muscle","myalgia"],["nephr/o, ren/o","kidney","nephrology, renal"],["neur/o","nerve","neuropathy"],["oste/o","bone","osteoporosis"],["pneum/o, pulmon/o","lung, air","pneumonia, pulmonary"],["thromb/o","clot","thrombosis"]]},
'Notice that several structures have two combining forms, one Greek and one Latin: *nephr/o* and *ren/o* for kidney, *hyster/o* and *uter/o* for uterus, *mast/o* and *mamm/o* for breast. The Greek form usually appears in disease and procedure names (*nephritis*, *mastectomy*), while the Latin form often appears in anatomical adjectives (*renal*, *mammary*). Knowing both prevents you from rejecting a correctly spelled option simply because it uses the less familiar root.']]
],takeaways:['A combining form is a root plus a combining vowel, usually o.','Drop the combining vowel before a vowel-initial suffix; keep it before a consonant-initial suffix and between roots.','Prefixes usually attach directly; confirm exceptions in Taber’s and log them.'],terms:[['Root','The core meaning of a medical term.'],['Combining vowel','A vowel, usually o, linking word parts.'],['Combining form','A root plus combining vowel, such as gastr/o.']],mcqs:[q('Inflammation of the stomach is spelled:','Gastritis','Gastroitis','Gasteritis','Gastritus','Drop the o before -itis.'),q('Visual examination of the stomach is spelled:','Gastroscopy','Gastrscopy','Gastoscopy','Gastroscopey','Keep the o before -scopy.'),q('Inflammation of the stomach and intestines is spelled:','Gastroenteritis','Gastrenteritis','Gastroenteroitis','Gastroentritis','Keep the vowel between roots; drop it before -itis.'),q('Surgical removal of a kidney is spelled:','Nephrectomy','Nephroectomy','Nefrectomy','Nephrectemy','Drop the o before -ectomy.'),q('Condition of kidney stones is spelled:','Nephrolithiasis','Nephrolithasis','Nephrolythiasis','Nefrolithiasis','Nephr/o + lith + -iasis.'),q('Formation of blood cells is spelled:','Hematopoiesis','Hematopoesis','Hemotopoiesis','Hematopoeisis','Hemat/o + -poiesis.'),q('Which suffix requires keeping the combining vowel?','-scopy','-itis','-ectomy','-algia','-scopy begins with a consonant.'),q('Enlargement of the heart is spelled:','Cardiomegaly','Cardimegaly','Cardiomegally','Cardiomeagaly','Keep the o before -megaly.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the rules for combining vowels using gastritis, gastroscopy and gastroenteritis as examples, and describe how you would handle a term that seems to break the rules.',rubric:[['Define root, combining vowel and combining form',['root','combining vowel','combining form','gastr']],['Apply vowel-initial and consonant-initial rules',['drop','keep','-itis','-scopy','vowel']],['Explain keeping vowel between roots',['between roots','enter','gastroenteritis']],['Handle exceptions with dictionary and log',['exception','taber','dictionary','log']]],model:'Medical terms combine a root such as gastr (stomach) with a combining vowel, usually o, to form a combining form, gastr/o. When the suffix begins with a vowel, the combining vowel drops, so gastr/o plus -itis is gastritis. When the suffix begins with a consonant, it stays, so gastr/o plus -scopy is gastroscopy. Between two roots the vowel stays even before a vowel, so gastr/o plus enter/o plus -itis is gastroenteritis, with the vowel kept between the roots and dropped before -itis. If a term seems to break the rules, I would confirm its spelling in Taber’s, note the reason if I can find one, and add it to my error log as an exception to review.'},sources:[RULES,MEDWORDS,MPLUS_DICT]};

export const S3={title:'Prefixes and suffixes that are easy to confuse',hook:'Hypo- and hyper- differ by one letter and mean opposite things. Gastrectomy removes the stomach; gastrotomy only cuts into it; gastrostomy creates an opening. Near-identical word parts produce many of the hardest Round One distractors, because each wrong choice is a real word part.',objectives:['Distinguish commonly confused prefixes by meaning and spelling.','Distinguish surgical and diagnostic suffixes such as -ectomy, -otomy, -ostomy, -scopy and -graphy.','Spell the -rrhea, -rrhagia, -rrhaphy and -rrhexis family.','Use definitions to choose between near-identical spellings.'],sections:[
['Opposite and look-alike prefixes',[
'Several prefix pairs differ by a letter or two but carry very different meanings. **Hyper-** means above or excessive (*hyperglycemia*, high blood sugar), while **hypo-** means below or deficient (*hypoglycemia*). **Inter-** means between (*intercostal*, between the ribs), while **intra-** means within (*intravenous*, within a vein). **Ante-** means before (*antepartum*), while **anti-** means against (*antibiotic*). **Peri-** means around (*pericardium*), while **para-** means beside, near or abnormal (*paranasal*, *paresthesia*). **Dys-** means painful, difficult or abnormal (*dysuria*), while **dis-** means apart or reversal (*dislocation*). **Ab-** means away from and **ad-** means toward, as in *abduction* and *adduction*.',
'For each pair, link the spelling to a memory anchor: hyp**o** has an **o** like “l**o**w”; int**ra** is “in”; ant**e** is “before” (e as in early); ant**i** is “against.” In Round One, read the definition first and decide the meaning before looking at the options, because the distractors are designed to look familiar.',
match('Match the prefix to its meaning',[['hyper-','Above or excessive'],['hypo-','Below or deficient'],['inter-','Between'],['intra-','Within'],['ante-','Before'],['anti-','Against'],['peri-','Around'],['dys-','Painful, difficult or abnormal']])]],
['Surgical suffixes: -ectomy, -otomy, -ostomy, -plasty',[
'The cutting suffixes are a classic source of errors. **-ectomy** means surgical removal (*appendectomy*, *cholecystectomy*). **-otomy** (or *-tomy*) means cutting into, an incision (*tracheotomy*, *craniotomy*). **-ostomy** (or *-stomy*) means creating a new opening (*colostomy*, *tracheostomy*). **-plasty** means surgical repair or reconstruction (*rhinoplasty*, *angioplasty*). **-rrhaphy** means suturing (*herniorrhaphy*). **-pexy** means surgical fixation (*nephropexy*), and **-desis** means surgical binding or fusion (*arthrodesis*). A useful chain: an incision (*-otomy*) may lead to removal (*-ectomy*), repair (*-plasty*) or a new opening (*-ostomy*).',
sort('Which surgical suffix fits?',['-ectomy (removal)','-otomy (incision)','-ostomy (new opening)','-plasty (repair)'],[['Removal of the appendix',0],['Removal of the gallbladder',0],['Incision into the skull',1],['Incision into the trachea',1],['Opening from the colon to the abdominal surface',2],['Opening created in the trachea',2],['Repair of the nose',3],['Repair of a blood vessel',3]])]],
['Diagnostic suffixes: -scopy, -graphy, -gram, -graph, -metry',[
'**-scopy** is the process of visual examination (*endoscopy*), and **-scope** is the instrument (*endoscope*). **-graphy** is the process of recording (*mammography*), **-gram** is the record produced (*mammogram*), and **-graph** is the instrument that records (*electrocardiograph*). **-metry** is the process of measuring (*spirometry*), and **-meter** is the measuring instrument (*spirometer*). Questions often test process versus product versus instrument: an *electrocardiogram* is the tracing; an *electrocardiograph* is the machine; *electrocardiography* is the procedure.',
'Other suffixes that look alike: **-plasia** (formation, development: *hyperplasia*) versus **-plasty** (repair) versus **-plegia** (paralysis: *hemiplegia*). **-penia** (deficiency: *leukopenia*) versus **-poiesis** (formation: *erythropoiesis*). **-lysis** (breakdown: *hemolysis*) versus **-ptysis** (spitting: *hemoptysis*).',
flip('Process, product or instrument?',[['-scopy','Process of visual examination'],['-scope','Instrument for viewing'],['-graphy','Process of recording'],['-gram','The record produced'],['-graph','Instrument that records'],['-metry','Process of measuring'],['-plegia','Paralysis'],['-ptysis','Spitting (as in hemoptysis)']])]],
['The rrh family',[
'Four suffixes share the letters *rrh*, from Greek words for flowing and bursting. **-rrhea** means flow or discharge (*diarrhea*, *rhinorrhea*, *amenorrhea*, *otorrhea*). **-rrhagia** or **-rrhage** means excessive flow, hemorrhage (*hemorrhage*, *menorrhagia*). **-rrhaphy** means suturing (*herniorrhaphy*). **-rrhexis** means rupture (*hysterorrhexis*, *angiorrhexis*). All begin with a doubled *r* followed by *h* when attached to a combining form or prefix ending in a vowel. Spell them as r–r–h, then the vowel: *-rrhea*, *-rrhagia*, *-rrhaphy*, *-rrhexis*.',
'The most common misspellings drop one *r* (*diarhea*), drop the *h* (*diarrea*) or misplace it (*diahrrea*). Say the pattern aloud—“double r, h”—every time you practice one of these words. Related terms keep the pattern: *hemorrhoid*, *seborrhea*, *pyorrhea*, *galactorrhea*.',
spell('Spell the rrh words',[['diarrhea','Frequent, loose or watery bowel movements'],['hemorrhage','Excessive or uncontrolled bleeding'],['rhinorrhea','Discharge from the nose'],['herniorrhaphy','Surgical repair of a hernia by suturing'],['amenorrhea','Absence of menstruation'],['menorrhagia','Excessive menstrual bleeding']])]],
['Strategy for near-identical options',[
'For each Round One item: read the definition, identify the parts it describes (location, organ, action), predict the spelling, then compare each option letter by letter against your prediction. Eliminate options with the wrong prefix meaning first, then the wrong suffix, then check letter sequence. If two options remain, look for double letters, silent letters and vowel order, which are the most common differences. In the spelldown, ask for the definition when a term could be a sound-alike: *ileum* and *ilium* sound the same but are spelled differently and refer to different structures.']],
['Number, size and position prefixes',[
'Another group of prefixes describes number, size and position. Their spelling is usually regular, but they combine with roots in ways that create familiar traps, such as *hemi-* versus *semi-* (both meaning half) and *endo-* versus *exo-*. Learn them as a set so you can decode and spell any term built from them.',
{t:'table',caption:"Prefixes of number, size and position",head:["Prefix","Meaning","Example term"],rows:[["uni-, mono-","one","unilateral, mononucleosis"],["bi-, di-","two","bilateral, diplopia"],["tri-","three","tricuspid"],["quadri-, tetra-","four","quadriplegia, tetralogy"],["hemi-, semi-","half","hemiplegia, semicircular"],["poly-, multi-","many","polyuria, multipara"],["oligo-","few, scanty","oliguria"],["pan-","all","pancytopenia"],["micro-, macro-","small, large","microcephaly, macrocytic"],["endo-, exo-","within, outside","endoscopy, exophthalmos"],["epi-","upon, over","epidermis"],["sub-, hypo-","under, below","subcutaneous, hypodermic"],["supra-, super-","above","suprapubic"],["retro-","behind, backward","retroperitoneal"],["trans-","across, through","transdermal"]]},
'Spelling tip: when a prefix ends in a vowel and the root begins with one, check whether the vowels stay. *Hemi-* + *anopia* becomes **hemianopia** (both vowels kept), while *poly-* + *uria* becomes **polyuria**. Prefixes rarely drop letters, so if an option looks shortened, such as *polyria*, it is probably wrong.']]
],takeaways:['Hyper/hypo, inter/intra, ante/anti, peri/para and dys/dis are high-yield pairs.','-ectomy removes, -otomy cuts into, -ostomy creates an opening, -plasty repairs, -rrhaphy sutures.','-scopy/-scope, -graphy/-gram/-graph and the rrh family are tested often; say “double r, h.”'],terms:[['-ectomy','Surgical removal.'],['-ostomy','Surgical creation of an opening.'],['-rrhexis','Rupture.']],mcqs:[q('Excessive menstrual bleeding is spelled:','Menorrhagia','Menorhagia','Menorrhagea','Menoragia','Men/o + -rrhagia.'),q('Frequent, watery bowel movements are spelled:','Diarrhea','Diarhea','Diarrea','Diahrrea','Dia- + -rrhea.'),q('Surgical removal of the gallbladder is:','Cholecystectomy','Cholecystotomy','Cholecystostomy','Cholecystoplasty','-ectomy means removal.'),q('Creation of an opening from the colon to the abdominal surface is:','Colostomy','Colectomy','Colotomy','Coloplasty','-ostomy creates an opening.'),q('The tracing produced by an electrocardiograph is an:','Electrocardiogram','Electrocardiograph','Electrocardiography','Electrocardioscope','-gram is the record.'),q('Within a vein is:','Intravenous','Intervenous','Intrevenous','Intravenus','Intra- means within.'),q('Paralysis of one side of the body is:','Hemiplegia','Hemiplasia','Hemiplegea','Hemiplagia','-plegia means paralysis.'),q('Spitting up blood from the respiratory tract is:','Hemoptysis','Hemolysis','Hemoptisis','Hemoptyses','-ptysis means spitting.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would choose the correct answer when a definition describes surgical removal of the appendix and the options include appendectomy, appendotomy, appendicectomy and appendostomy. Then explain the rrh family with examples.',rubric:[['Identify meaning of -ectomy versus -otomy and -ostomy',['ectomy','removal','otomy','incision','ostomy','opening']],['Choose the correct U.S. spelling with reasoning',['appendectomy','dictionary','taber']],['Explain rrh suffixes',['rrhea','rrhagia','rrhaphy','rrhexis']],['Give spelling pattern and examples',['double r','diarrhea','hemorrhage']]],model:'I would read the definition first: surgical removal means the suffix must be -ectomy, which eliminates appendotomy (incision) and appendostomy (creating an opening). Of the remaining options, appendectomy is the standard U.S. spelling found in Taber’s; appendicectomy is a variant used in some other countries, so in a U.S. competition I would choose appendectomy. The rrh family comes from Greek words for flowing and bursting: -rrhea means discharge (diarrhea, rhinorrhea), -rrhagia or -rrhage means excessive flow or bleeding (menorrhagia, hemorrhage), -rrhaphy means suturing (herniorrhaphy) and -rrhexis means rupture. All begin with a double r followed by h, so I say “double r, h” when spelling them.'},sources:[RULES,MEDWORDS,TABERS]};

export const S4={title:'Greek letter patterns: silent letters and unusual beginnings',hook:'Pneumonia begins with a silent p. Psychiatry begins with ps. Rhinitis hides an h after its r, and xerostomia starts with an x that sounds like z. Greek-derived spellings preserve letters English speakers no longer pronounce, which is why they appear so often in spelling competitions.',objectives:['Recognize silent-letter beginnings such as pn, ps, pt, mn, gn and rh.','Spell sounds represented by unusual letters: ph for f, ch for k, x for z, y for i.','Recognize vowel combinations such as ae, oe and eu and their U.S. forms.','Use sound-to-letter patterns to predict and verify spellings.'],sections:[
['Silent first letters',[
'Several Greek combinations begin with a letter that is silent in English. **pn-** appears in terms about air or lungs: *pneumonia*, *pneumothorax*, *pneumatic*, and in breathing terms such as *dyspnea* and *apnea*, where the p is pronounced in the middle of the word. **ps-** appears in *psychiatry*, *psychology*, *psoriasis* and *pseudocyst*. **pt-** appears in *ptosis* (drooping) and *ptyalism* (excess saliva). **mn-** appears in *mnemonic*. **gn-** appears in *gnathic* (jaw), though in the middle of words such as *prognosis* and *diagnosis* the g is pronounced. When you hear a term beginning with an n, s or t sound related to lungs, mind or drooping, consider a silent p.',
'Silent letters also occur inside words: the *h* in *rheumatoid*, the *g* in *diaphragm* and *phlegm*, and the *c* in *muscle* are all silent. Practice by saying the word as it sounds, then spelling it with the silent letters stressed, a technique called **spelling pronunciation**: “p-NEU-monia.”',
sort('Which silent beginning?',['pn-','ps-','pt-','rh-'],[['Inflammation of the lungs',0],['Collapsed lung from air in the pleural space',0],['Chronic skin disease with scaly patches',1],['Branch of medicine concerned with mental disorders',1],['Drooping of the upper eyelid',2],['Excessive salivation',2],['Inflammation of the nasal mucous membrane',3],['Autoimmune joint disease',3]])]],
['Letters that make other sounds',[
'**ph** spells the f sound in Greek words: *pharynx*, *esophagus*, *phlebotomy*, *nephron*, *lymph*, *ophthalmology*. If an f-sounding medical term comes from Greek, it is usually ph. **ch** spells a k sound: *cholecystitis*, *chondromalacia*, *chronic*, *chemotherapy*, *tachycardia*, *ischemia*. **x** at the start sounds like z: *xerostomia* (dry mouth), *xiphoid* (sword-shaped process of the sternum), *xanthoma*. **y** often spells a short i sound: *dysphagia*, *hypoxia*, *myocardium*, *cyst*, *gynecology*, *polyp*. **rh** spells an r with a silent h: *rhinitis*, *rheumatoid*, *rhabdomyolysis*, *rhythm*.',
'Combine patterns carefully. *Ophthalmology* contains ph-th-al: o-p-h-t-h-a-l-m-o-l-o-g-y. Many spellers omit the first h (*opthalmology*), which is one of the most common competition errors. Break it into *ophthalm/o* (eye) + *-logy*. Similarly, *diaphragm* is d-i-a-p-h-r-a-g-m with a silent g, and *phlegm* is p-h-l-e-g-m.',
spell('Spell the Greek patterns',[['ophthalmology','Branch of medicine dealing with the eye'],['pneumothorax','Air in the pleural space causing lung collapse'],['psoriasis','A chronic skin disease with red, scaly patches'],['xerostomia','Dryness of the mouth'],['rhabdomyolysis','Breakdown of skeletal muscle tissue'],['diaphragm','The muscle separating the chest and abdominal cavities']])]],
['Vowel combinations and U.S. spellings',[
'British and older texts keep the Greek and Latin vowel pairs *ae* and *oe*: *anaemia*, *haemorrhage*, *oedema*, *oesophagus*, *paediatric*, *orthopaedic*. Standard U.S. spelling usually simplifies them to a single *e*: **anemia**, **hemorrhage**, **edema**, **esophagus**, **pediatric**, **orthopedic**. Some words keep the pair even in U.S. usage, such as *aerobic* and *aeruginosa* in organism names, and plural endings like *vertebrae* keep *ae*. HOSA notes that a few terms have two accepted spellings, such as *orthopedic* and *orthopaedic* or *mammaplasty* and *mammoplasty*, and judges receive both. When a list allows variants, either is accepted, but learning the standard U.S. form is safest.',
'The combination **eu** means good or normal and keeps both letters: *euthyroid*, *euphoria*, *euthanasia*, *eupnea*. **ei** and **ie** appear in *leukocyte* (eu, not ue), *hematopoiesis* (ie), and *seizure* (ei). Vowel order is a frequent source of sequence errors, so mark it in your notes.',
match('Match the British form to the U.S. spelling',[['anaemia','anemia'],['oedema','edema'],['oesophagus','esophagus'],['paediatric','pediatric'],['haemorrhage','hemorrhage'],['gynaecology','gynecology']])]],
['Predict, then verify',[
'When you hear an unfamiliar term, use sound-to-letter patterns to generate likely spellings: an f sound in a Greek medical word suggests ph; a k sound suggests ch or c; a z sound at the start suggests x; an n sound at the start of a lung term suggests pn. Then verify in Taber’s and add the term to your pattern list. Over time you will have lists for each pattern, which are more efficient to review than random word lists.',
'In the spelldown, if you are unsure whether a term begins with a silent letter, request the definition. A definition mentioning lungs or air supports pn-; one mentioning the mind supports psych-. You still cannot see the word, but meaning narrows the likely spelling. Keep calm: you have two minutes and can request the pronunciation again before you begin spelling.',
flip('Sound-to-letter patterns',[['f sound in a Greek term','ph (pharynx, lymph)'],['k sound','ch (cholecystitis, chronic)'],['z sound at the start','x (xerostomia, xiphoid)'],['n sound at the start of a lung term','pn (pneumonia)'],['s sound at the start of a mind term','ps (psychiatry)'],['short i sound','often y (dysphagia, hypoxia)']])]],
['Pattern reference list',[
'Build your own pattern lists from the examples below and add new terms as you meet them. Grouping by pattern is more efficient than memorizing alphabetical lists, because one rule covers every word in the group. Review the list aloud, stressing the unusual letters: “o-p-H-t-H-almology.”',
{t:'table',caption:"Unusual letter patterns and examples",head:["Pattern","Sound","Examples"],rows:[["pn-","n","pneumonia, pneumothorax, pneumococcus"],["ps-","s","psychiatry, psoriasis, pseudocyst, psychosis"],["pt-","t","ptosis, ptyalism"],["rh-","r","rhinitis, rheumatoid, rhabdomyolysis, rhinoplasty"],["ph","f","pharynx, phlebotomy, nephron, lymphoma, ophthalmic"],["ch","k","cholecystitis, chondroma, chronic, ischemia, tachypnea"],["x- (initial)","z","xerostomia, xiphoid, xanthoma"],["y","i","dysphagia, hypoxia, cyst, gynecology, myocardium"],["eu","yoo","leukocyte, pleurisy, euthyroid, aneurysm"],["silent g","—","diaphragm, phlegm"]]},
'Some patterns stack within a single word. *Pneumonoultramicroscopicsilicovolcanoconiosis*, often cited as the longest dictionary word, begins with silent-p **pn**, but competition lists rely on far more practical terms. More useful stacked examples are **sphygmomanometer** (ph, y, silent-looking g), **ophthalmoscope** (ph-th) and **rhinopharyngitis** (rh, ph, y). Spell each chunk separately to keep the patterns in order.']]
],takeaways:['Silent beginnings: pn (lungs), ps (mind, skin), pt (drooping), rh (nose, joints).','ph = f, ch = k, initial x = z, y = short i in many Greek terms.','U.S. spelling usually simplifies ae and oe to e; learn the U.S. standard and note accepted variants.'],terms:[['Spelling pronunciation','Saying a word with its silent letters to remember them.'],['Ptosis','Drooping, especially of the upper eyelid.'],['Xerostomia','Dryness of the mouth.']],mcqs:[q('Inflammation of the lungs is spelled:','Pneumonia','Neumonia','Pnuemonia','Pneumonnia','Silent p, then eu.'),q('The branch of medicine dealing with the eye is:','Ophthalmology','Opthalmology','Ophthamology','Opthamology','o-p-h-t-h-a-l-m.'),q('A chronic skin disease with scaly patches is:','Psoriasis','Soriasis','Psoriasus','Psorasis','Silent p.'),q('Drooping of the upper eyelid is:','Ptosis','Tosis','Ptossis','Phtosis','Silent p in pt.'),q('Dryness of the mouth is:','Xerostomia','Zerostomia','Xerostomea','Xerostomnia','Initial x sounds like z.'),q('The U.S. spelling of oedema is:','Edema','Oedema','Odema','Eedema','U.S. usage simplifies oe to e.'),q('Breakdown of skeletal muscle tissue is:','Rhabdomyolysis','Rabdomyolysis','Rhabdomyalysis','Rhabdomyolisis','rh + abd + my/o + -lysis.'),q('Difficulty swallowing is:','Dysphagia','Disphagia','Dysfagia','Dysphasia','Dys- + phag (eat) + -ia; dysphasia is a speech disorder.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain three Greek spelling patterns that cause errors, with two correctly spelled examples of each, and describe how you would use these patterns in the spelldown when you are unsure of a term.',rubric:[['Explain silent-letter beginnings with examples',['pn','ps','pt','silent','pneumonia','psoriasis']],['Explain ph, ch, x or y sound patterns with examples',['ph','ch','x','y','ophthalmology','cholecystitis']],['Explain ae/oe or vowel patterns',['ae','oe','anemia','edema','u.s.']],['Spelldown strategy',['definition','pronunciation','predict','verify']]],model:'Silent first letters come from Greek: pn appears in lung terms such as pneumonia and pneumothorax, and ps appears in psychiatry and psoriasis. Greek ph spells an f sound, as in pharynx and ophthalmology, and ch spells a k sound, as in cholecystitis and chronic. Older British spellings use ae and oe, but U.S. spellings simplify them, as in anemia and edema. In the spelldown, if I am unsure whether a term begins with a silent letter, I would request the definition, because a reference to lungs suggests pn and a reference to the mind suggests ps; I could also request another pronunciation before starting, then spell carefully using the pattern I predicted.'},sources:[RULES,MEDWORDS,TABERS]};

export const S5={title:'Doubled letters, vowel order and other sequence traps',hook:'Accommodation, occlusion, appendicitis, staphylococcus, abscess—each contains a doubled letter that spellers often drop, and each has a neighbor that does not double. Sequence errors are the most common kind of misspelling, and they respond best to chunking and pattern lists.',objectives:['Explain why doubled consonants appear at prefix and root boundaries.','Spell high-frequency medical terms with doubled letters.','Identify terms that are often wrongly doubled.','Use chunking to control letter sequence in long terms.'],sections:[
['Why letters double',[
'Many doubled consonants occur where a prefix ending in a consonant meets a root beginning with the same letter. *Ad-* + *duct* gives **adduction**; *in-* + *nervate* gives **innervation**; *col-* (with) + *lateral* gives **collateral**; *ac-* (a form of ad-) + *commodation* gives **accommodation**. Latin prefixes often changed their final consonant to match the next letter, a process called **assimilation**: *ad-* becomes *ac-*, *af-*, *ag-*, *al-*, *ap-* or *as-*, producing *accretion*, *afferent*, *aggregation*, *allergen*, *apposition* and *assimilation*. Knowing the prefix explains the double.',
'Other doubles come from roots that already contain them: *-rrh-* suffixes, *staphyl/o* and *cocc/i* (*staphylococcus*), *append* (*appendicitis*), *occlud* (*occlusion*), *abscess*. Some come from adding a suffix to a root ending in a single consonant, but medical terms do not follow ordinary English doubling rules consistently, so learn high-frequency words individually.',
match('Explain the double letter',[['adduction','ad- (toward) + duction'],['innervation','in- + nerv-'],['afferent','af- (assimilated ad-) + ferent'],['accommodation','ac- (assimilated ad-) + commodation'],['collateral','col- (with) + lateral'],['staphylococcus','staphyl/o + cocc + -us']])]],
['High-frequency doubled terms',[
'Some terms appear on spelling lists again and again. Memorize the doubled letters in each: **abscess** (two pairs: bs, then ss—a-b-s-c-e-s-s), **accommodation** (double c, double m), **anastomosis** (no doubles—often wrongly doubled), **appendicitis** (double p), **apparatus** (double p), **arrhythmia** (double r with h, and y), **cirrhosis** (double r with h), **coccyx** (double c, then y and x), **desiccation** (one s, double c), **diarrhea** (double r with h), **immunization** (double m), **occult** (double c), **occlusion** (double c), **palliative** (double l), **parallel** (double l, then single l), **sessile** (double s), **staphylococcus** (single ph, double c), **succinylcholine** (double c), **tonsillectomy** (double l), **varicella** (double l).',
'Group them by the doubled letter and practice one group per day. Say the double out loud when you spell: “a-p-p, double p.” Because spelldown judges write each letter as you say it, the clearest habit is to say each letter separately—“p, p”—so there is no doubt about what you spelled.',
spell('Spell the doubled-letter terms',[['abscess','A localized collection of pus surrounded by inflamed tissue'],['accommodation','Adjustment of the eye for seeing at different distances'],['arrhythmia','An irregular heartbeat'],['tonsillectomy','Surgical removal of the tonsils'],['staphylococcus','A genus of spherical bacteria that grow in clusters'],['cirrhosis','Chronic liver disease with scarring']])]],
['Terms that should NOT double',[
'Overcorrection is also an error. Common wrongly doubled terms include **anastomosis**, **embolism**, **hemolysis**, **dysphagia**, **parenteral**, **pericardium** (one r), **sacral** (one c), **syncope**, **tracheal** and **vesicle**. By contrast, **villus** and **villi** really do contain a double l—an example of why each term must be checked rather than guessed. The word **desiccate** has one s and two c’s, the reverse of what many people expect. **Necessary** is ordinary English but appears in medical writing with one c and two s’s.',
'Notice that some terms contain one doubled pair and one single letter that looks like it should double, such as *occasional* or *necessary*. The best defense is to study terms in contrasting pairs and mark the double: *anastomosis* (none) versus *accommodation* (two pairs).',
sort('Double letter or not?',['Contains a doubled consonant','No doubled consonant'],[['appendicitis',0],['palliative',0],['coccyx',0],['immunization',0],['anastomosis',1],['syncope',1],['pericardium',1],['embolism',1]])]],
['Vowel order and chunking',[
'Vowel order errors are as common as doubling errors: *leukocyte* (eu), *pleurisy* (eu), *hematopoiesis* (oie), *tuberculosis*, *auscultation* (au, then ul), *aneurysm* (eu, then y), *pharyngeal* (y, then ea), *bronchiectasis* (ie then a), *eczema* (e-c-z). Write the vowel pattern separately to study it: *aneurysm* is a-n-**eu**-r-**y**-s-m.',
'**Chunking** breaks a long term into meaningful or pronounceable units, each short enough to hold in working memory. *Electroencephalography* becomes electr/o – encephal/o – graphy; *sphygmomanometer* becomes sphygm/o (pulse) – man/o (pressure) – meter; *cholangiopancreatography* becomes chol – angi/o – pancreat/o – graphy. Spell one chunk, pause mentally, then the next. In the spelldown you have two minutes, so there is time to spell deliberately chunk by chunk.',
order('Spell sphygmomanometer by chunks',['s-p-h-y-g-m (sphygm: pulse)','o (combining vowel)','m-a-n (man: pressure)','o (combining vowel)','m-e-t-e-r (instrument for measuring)'])]],
['Building your doubled-letter list',[
'Create three running lists in your error log: terms with doubles, terms often wrongly doubled, and terms with tricky vowel order. Add every new example you meet in practice. Review the lists with a partner who reads definitions while you spell aloud, then switch roles. Use the spelling studio to check letter-by-letter differences, which show exactly where a double was dropped or added. Revisit the lists the week before competition, because sequence errors are the easiest points to recover with focused review.']],
['Endings that trip spellers',[
'English suffixes that sound alike also cause errors in medical terms. The endings **-ance** and **-ence** sound the same: *resistance*, *compliance* and *tolerance* use -ance, while *incontinence*, *competence*, *occurrence* and *dependence* use -ence. The endings **-able** and **-ible** also sound alike: *absorbable* and *treatable* use -able, while *compatible*, *reversible*, *flexible* and *contagious*-related *transmissible* use -ible. The endings **-sion** and **-tion** differ by pattern: *incision*, *excision*, *infusion*, *effusion* and *occlusion* use -sion (often from verbs ending in -de or -se), while *palpation*, *auscultation*, *dilation* and *ventilation* use -tion.',
{t:'table',caption:"Sound-alike endings",head:["Ending","Examples"],rows:[["-ance","resistance, compliance, tolerance"],["-ence","incontinence, competence, dependence, occurrence"],["-able","absorbable, treatable, palpable"],["-ible","compatible, reversible, flexible, transmissible"],["-sion","incision, excision, infusion, effusion, occlusion"],["-tion","palpation, auscultation, ventilation, dilation"]]},
'There is no single reliable rule for these endings, so learn high-frequency medical words individually and add misses to your error log. Notice related forms: *incise* becomes *incision*, *occlude* becomes *occlusion*, and *infuse* becomes *infusion*, which supports the -sion pattern for verbs ending in -de or -se.']]
],takeaways:['Doubled consonants often mark prefix–root boundaries and assimilated prefixes (ad- → ac-, af-, al-, ap-).','Memorize high-frequency doubles (abscess, accommodation, appendicitis, staphylococcus) and non-doubles (anastomosis, syncope).','Use chunking and vowel-pattern notes for long terms.'],terms:[['Assimilation','A prefix’s final consonant changing to match the next letter, creating a double.'],['Chunking','Breaking a long term into short, meaningful units.'],['Anastomosis','A surgical or natural connection between two tubular structures.']],mcqs:[q('A localized collection of pus is spelled:','Abscess','Abcess','Absess','Abscesss','a-b-s-c-e-s-s.'),q('An irregular heartbeat is spelled:','Arrhythmia','Arhythmia','Arrythmia','Arrhythmea','Double r, h, then y.'),q('A surgical connection between two tubular structures is:','Anastomosis','Annastomosis','Anastamosis','Anastommosis','No doubled letters.'),q('Inflammation of the appendix is spelled:','Appendicitis','Apendicitis','Appendecitis','Appendicitus','Double p.'),q('Sudden fainting due to reduced blood flow to the brain is:','Syncope','Syncopy','Sincope','Synccope','No double letters; ends in -ope.'),q('A dilated, weakened area of an artery wall is spelled:','Aneurysm','Aneurism','Anuerysm','Aneurysim','a-n-eu-r-y-s-m.'),q('An instrument for measuring blood pressure is:','Sphygmomanometer','Sphygmonanometer','Sphygmomanometre','Sphigmomanometer','sphygm/o + man/o + -meter.'),q('Which term contains an assimilated prefix creating a double letter?','Afferent','Anastomosis','Syncope','Embolism','af- is an assimilated form of ad-.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain why many medical terms contain doubled consonants, give three correctly spelled examples with the reason for each double, name two terms that are often wrongly doubled, and describe how you would use chunking for a long term.',rubric:[['Explain prefix boundaries and assimilation',['prefix','assimil','ad-','boundary']],['Three doubled examples with reasons',['accommodation','adduction','appendicitis','staphylococcus','collateral','abscess']],['Two wrongly doubled terms',['anastomosis','syncope','pericardium','embolism']],['Chunking explanation with example',['chunk','sphygmomanometer','electroencephalography','part']]],model:'Doubled consonants often occur where a prefix meets a root beginning with the same letter, and Latin prefixes such as ad- assimilated their last letter to the next one. Adduction is ad- plus duction; accommodation contains ac-, an assimilated ad-, plus commodation; and collateral is col- (with) plus lateral. Some doubles come from roots, such as cocc in staphylococcus. Anastomosis and syncope are often wrongly doubled but contain no doubled consonants. For a long term such as sphygmomanometer, I would chunk it into sphygm (pulse), o, man (pressure), o and meter, spelling one chunk at a time.'},sources:[RULES,MEDWORDS,MPLUS_DICT]};
